A||||2||merlin||The dreaded wheel alignment||||1||
Z||000000||merlin||01-21-2001||01:07 PM||||I apologize for not knowing allot about wheel alignments, but I do know that one of my real wheels on my 87 GT needs to have the tow-in/tow-out adjusted.  I’ve read some horror stories about alignment shops not doing the job correctly on Fieros.  Is there any rule of thumb to follow when selecting an alignment shop.  Have people had decent success with Pontiac dealers as opposed to other shops. ||||reg||1
Z||000001||2birds||01-21-2001||02:12 PM||||Things I have learned...<P>A 4 wheel alignment should run $50 to $70 max, even in the Northeast.<P>First this assumes that all parts are in good shape.  This includes all balljoints, tie rod ends, and control arm and cradle bushings.  Labor isn't cheap for any this, and markup is even worse.  Some shops will give you a courtesy check for less money or even free, but it also gives them the chance to sell you more stuff.  Ask for a second shop's opinion either way.  Same as brake shops.<P>For 84-87, the front camber is not adjustable unless you have aftermarket slotted balljoints (Moog, et al) installed.  There is a bit of adjustment that can be done by turning the uppers 180 degrees, but the labor alone for that costs more than brand new joints, and it may or may not fix it.<P>Front end caster is adjusted by varying the thickness of the washers on the upper control arm bushing shaft.  I have no idea what this will cost for the parts, but I'd check various parts outlets beforehand so the alignment shop can't jam you too hard when they mark them up for resale.  I've never needed them yet in thirteen years and 7 alignments.<P>Toe is the same for front and rear; real simple to adjust and no extra parts should be required.<P>Rear caster is not adjusted.<P>Rear camber is adjustable.  Aftermarket struts have an elongated slot in the lower bolt hole, whch allows the strut and spindle to pivot around the upper, changing the camber.  Factory GM struts don't have this slot, so they make a "camber kit", which I believe is just thinner bolts to give it some play.  If you have aftermarket struts, you should not need these, although I had one shop try to sell me a set anyway (more profit for them).  <P>Some alignment machines tell the tech what parts to put in and they follow this blindly, since most have not ever worked on a Fiero before and aren't in tune with the quirks associated with them.  An example would be the rear camber kits I mentioned above, or when the machine told the tech to install camber adjustment washers on the upper front control arm shaft mounting pad (only works on 88's, and most other older SLA type suspensions).  Just keep in mind that the tech may not be all that knowledgable.<P>Another thing is the cost.  Some will try to charge you extra for labor to install or move extra washers and rotate the ball joints. One tried to flat rate the whole thing at $55 an hour, since my front end is fully adjustable.  I shopped around until I found a shop that charged me $55 for the alignment, just like they advertised, no matter how long it took to get it right, and they gave me before and after printouts, and let me "assist/correct the technician" as required.   <P>Also check the alignment rack to be sure your car will not be damaged going on or off the rack. Many are too tall, especially if yours is lowered like mine.<P>I found mine at Sears, by the way.  I'll go back there again, because of the last experience.  It didn't hurt that I also bought four new tires at the same time.  I warned them ahead of time about it, and I went on a Thursday evening, so they were really slow and had the time to do it right.<P>There's my $10.02.<P><p>[This message has been edited by 2birds (edited 01-21-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||Motorhead||01-22-2001||05:44 PM||||I'm gonna' have to blow a horn for Merchant tire here.  They're nationwide, east coast anyway, and all my cars have gone there for the past ten years for alignment and tires.  I've lived all over the east coast and have never been dissappointed by the service at any of their shops.  Hell!  They even alligned my TR6 for less than $70.00 without damaging the car.<BR>Mike<P>------------------<BR>"The one who dies with the most toys wins!"<BR>  bluewater46@hotmail.com<BR>1986 coupe<BR>1984 coupe<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1372350&a=10234998&p=38686771&Sequence=0"> ||||reg||1
